@only-if workflows
# __NAME__ infrastructure

Two Terraform roots: `foundation` (slow, shared resources) and `instance` (per-deployment resources). See
`azure:structure` for why they're split. Both are driven by the app profile at `infra/app.json`, written by
the app stack; `azure:scaffold` turns it into the workload, host and database resources it asks for. Run
`python -B <scaffold-skill-dir>/scripts/scaffold.py options --schema` to see azure's own, app-agnostic
options as JSON.

## Bootstrap

Foundation starts with local state, using a personal Azure account kept isolated from any other CLI session:

```sh
export AZURE_CONFIG_DIR=$(mktemp -d)
az login
cd infra/foundation
terraform init
terraform apply -var-file=../env/dev.tfvars
```

Then migrate into the state storage account `apply` just created:

```sh
cat > backend.auto.tfvars.json <<JSON
{}
JSON
terraform init -migrate-state \
  -backend-config="resource_group_name=$(terraform output -raw resource_group_name)" \
  -backend-config="storage_account_name=$(terraform output -raw state_storage_account_name)" \
  -backend-config="container_name=tfstate" \
  -backend-config="key=__NAME__/foundation.tfstate"
```

`instance` uses the same account with key `__NAME__/instance.tfstate`.

## Repository variables

The workflow in `.github/workflows/infra.yml` reads these GitHub Actions variables, set once the foundation
has deployed:

- `AZURE_FOUNDATION_READY` — `"true"` once the state account above exists; gates the `plan` and `deploy`
  jobs.
- `AZURE_CLIENT_ID` — the deploy identity's client id (`terraform output -raw deploy_identity_client_id`).
- `AZURE_TENANT_ID`, `AZURE_SUBSCRIPTION_ID` — the target tenant and subscription.
- `TFSTATE_RESOURCE_GROUP`, `TFSTATE_STORAGE_ACCOUNT` — the values used in the migration above.

Update `infra/env/dev.tfvars` with the real GitHub owner, repository and their numeric ids:

```sh
gh api repos/<owner>/<repo> --jq '.owner.id, .id'
```

The first line is `github_owner_id`, the second is `github_repository_id`.

The federated credentials expect GitHub's immutable OIDC subject, `repo:<owner>@<owner-id>/<repo>@<repo-id>:...`,
which repositories created after 2026-07-15 send by default. Opt an older repository into immutable subjects
in its Actions OIDC settings before the first plan, or no login will match.

Service Bus subscriptions keep their `$Default` rule in this version; remove it by hand if a subscription
must receive only its `handled-types` messages.

## The deploy job

On push to `main`, `deploy` applies foundation, then for every workload in the profile runs its
`build.image` or `build.package` command — whichever its host needs — with `REGISTRY` (the registry login
server), `REPOSITORY` (`<name>-<id>`), `TAG` (the commit sha) and, for an image build,
`IMAGE=$REGISTRY/$REPOSITORY:$TAG` set in its environment. The database's `migration.build.image`, if any,
builds the same way under key `migration`. It then applies the migration job and its identity, admin and
database, runs the job and waits for it to succeed, applies the full instance plan with the built images,
deploys each package (Flex Consumption via `az functionapp deploy`; App Service via a staging-slot deploy,
a readiness wait, then a slot swap), and waits for each Container App's revision to become ready.

## Offline gate

Run this for each root before pushing, and in CI without any Azure login:

```sh
terraform fmt -check -recursive
terraform init -backend=false
terraform validate
```
